.TH HASHNUM 1 "2026" "hashnum @VERSION@" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
hashnum \- move a #N tag to the front of a filename
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B hashnum
[\fIOPTION\fR]... [\fIFILE\fR]...
.SH DESCRIPTION
Downloads and exports often name files like
.RB \(lq "Some Long Title #12.mp4" \(rq,
which sorts by title rather than by number.
.B hashnum
lifts the \fB#N\fR tag out of wherever it sits and puts it at the front:
.PP
.RS
.EX
Some Long Title #12.mp4   \->   #12 Some Long Title.mp4
.EE
.RE
.PP
With no arguments it works on every file in the current directory that
contains a \fB#N\fR tag. Files without one are left alone, and so are
directories. Whitespace left behind by the removed tag is tidied up, and an
existing file is never overwritten \(em a collision is reported and skipped.
.PP
Pair it with
.BR padnum (1)
when the numbers also need zero\-padding.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-n ", " \-\-dry\-run
Show what would be renamed and change nothing.
.TP
.BR \-q ", " \-\-quiet
Print nothing except errors.
.TP
.BR \-V ", " \-\-version
Print the version and exit.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Show a usage summary and exit.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
At least one file was renamed.
.TP
.B 1
Nothing matched.
.TP
.B 2
Bad usage.
.SH EXAMPLES
Look first, then do it:
.PP
.RS
.EX
hashnum \-n
hashnum
.EE
.RE
.PP
Rename, then pad the numbers to a common width:
.PP
.RS
.EX
hashnum && padnum
.EE
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR padnum (1),
.BR mv (1)
.SH AUTHOR
Written by @MAINTAINER@.
